  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nRest of Today into Tonight... Clear skies today have allowed \nfor temperatures to rise and highs are in the lower 60s this \nafternoon. We have also seen some periods of gusty winds \nassociated with the passage of both a warm and cold front today.\nTonight with continued clear skies and a post cold frontal \nairmass temperatures will fall into the 30s.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday... Two shortwaves will cross, one to the \nnorth and another to the south late tomorrow into Wednesday. \nThis will be the main synoptic driver of the period. The first \nwave will be across southern Canada and the second will be \nacross Iowa. The one more likely to drive forcing for us is the \nnorthern one. This will provide for some broad forcing across \nnorthern Minnesota and Wisconsin. More specifically this will \ngenerally be along and north of I-94. As mentioned in the past \ndiscussion, moisture is limited thanks to this other system to \nour south cutting off an important moisture source. Also the \nbetter setup for now appears to be across northern Minnesota \nrather than the central Minnesota counties we cover. So the \nexpectation will be for some very light rain adding up to five \nhundredths or less. This is also reflecting within the global \nensembles with central Minnesota to northern parts of west \ncentral Wisconsin seeing good agreement on rain, but very low \nQPF. We will see a cooling trend Tuesday and Wednesday with \ntemperatures still holding above normal. Highs tomorrow in the \n50s and then down into the mid 40s to around 50 on Wednesday. \nThe main push of cold air comes late week, when we drop below \nour normal mid 40s highs.\n\nThursday into the weekend... High pressure will move in clearing\nthe skies by Friday and continuing into the weekend. This will\nalso bring in cold air to end the week. Cold air advection will\nbe present Thursday and Friday keeping temperatures below\nnormal. This means highs upper 30s to lower 40s and lows in the\nmid 20s to lower 30s. This cold air mass will move out this\nweekend allowing for highs to rebound into the mid 40s to lower\n50s. As a reminder, normal highs this time of year are in the \nmid 40s. So this is a return to above normal. Little chance for \nany precipitation as a ridge moves in from the west.",
